WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.338
RTIC is used to structure embedded Rust
firmware around tasks,

2
00:00:03.418 --> 00:00:06.096
priorities, resources, and
interrupt-driven execution.

3
00:00:06.176 --> 00:00:09.764
That structure can make concurrent
firmware easier to reason about because the

4
00:00:09.844 --> 00:00:13.646
program describes execution contexts and
shared resources more explicitly than ad

5
00:00:13.726 --> 00:00:14.636
hoc interrupt code.

6
00:00:14.716 --> 00:00:17.233
Security in this setting is broader than
memory safety.

7
00:00:17.313 --> 00:00:19.723
Real-time firmware must remain available,
bounded,

8
00:00:19.803 --> 00:00:23.659
and predictable under normal operation,
degraded operation, and unexpected input.

9
00:00:23.739 --> 00:00:26.416
A task that runs too long, a queue that
grows without policy,

10
00:00:26.496 --> 00:00:30.084
or a debug path that remains broader than
intended can become a product security

11
00:00:30.164 --> 00:00:30.593
concern.

12
00:00:30.673 --> 00:00:33.672
Rust helps with memory safety in safe
code, ownership, borrowing,

13
00:00:33.752 --> 00:00:34.876
and type-driven design.

14
00:00:34.956 --> 00:00:37.313
RTIC helps organize concurrent embedded
behavior.

15
00:00:37.393 --> 00:00:40.552
Neither one automatically validates input,
proves timing behavior,

16
00:00:40.632 --> 00:00:43.952
protects secrets, secures updates, or
documents hardware assumptions.

17
00:00:44.032 --> 00:00:46.871
Those responsibilities still belong to the
engineering team.

18
00:00:46.951 --> 00:00:49.789
Interrupt-driven systems need especially
clear boundaries.

19
00:00:49.869 --> 00:00:52.654
Reviewers should understand which task
handles which event,

20
00:00:52.734 --> 00:00:55.090
which resources it can access, what work
is bounded,

21
00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:56.776
what happens when input is invalid,

22
00:00:56.856 --> 00:00:59.962
and how the system behaves when timing or
resource pressure appears.

23
00:01:00.042 --> 00:01:03.095
The defensive goal is field-ready firmware
whose behavior is safe,

24
00:01:03.175 --> 00:01:05.317
bounded, documented, tested, and
reviewable.

25
00:01:05.397 --> 00:01:08.182
RTIC can support that goal when task
design, priority design,

26
00:01:08.262 --> 00:01:12.010
resource ownership, hardware access,
updates, debug policy, dependency review,

27
00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:14.500
and release discipline are treated as one
lifecycle.
